Question 3 — L5 Interview
      The interviewer asks:      
              What is the time complexity and space complexity of this function?
      
      And more importantly:      
            Why is the space complexity O(n) even though we aren't explicitly creating an array, HashMap, or other data structure?

I'd make one small refinement for L5 precision:

    Time complexity is O(n) because there are n recursive calls, and each call performs O(1) work.
    
    Auxiliary space is O(n) because recursion uses the JVM call stack.
    Before the base case is reached, there can be up to n active stack frames simultaneously.

⭐ L5 phrasing
  "The auxiliary space is O(n), not because of an explicit data structure, but because the recursion depth is O(n). 
    Each recursive invocation creates a stack frame, and 
    these frames remain active until the base case is reached and the recursion starts unwinding."
================================================================================================================================================
2. Time Complexity

Let's define:   T(n) = time taken by solve(n)

Each call does constant work plus two recursive calls:   T(n) = 2T(n - 1) + O(1)
SO now; 
        T(n)
        = 2T(n-1) + 1
        = 2[2T(n-2) + 1] + 1
        = 4T(n-2) + 2 + 1
        = 8T(n-3) + 4 + 2 + 1
        ...
After n levels:   T(n) ≈ 2^n

Therefore:  Time Complexity = O(2ⁿ)

Another way to understand O(2ⁿ)

Look at the number of calls at each level.  1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 2ⁿ
This geometric series is:   2ⁿ⁺¹ - 1

Dropping constants:    O(2ⁿ)

So the exponential complexity comes from the branching factor of 2.
================================================================================================================================================
4. Now the important part: Space Complexity
        
        This is where people commonly make a mistake.
        
        You might think:   "There are O(2ⁿ) calls, so space must also be O(2ⁿ)."
        
                    No.
        
        The calls don't all exist simultaneously.
        
        The JVM executes one branch deeply before coming back and executing the other branch.
        
        For example:     solve(3)
                            |
                         solve(2)
                            |
                         solve(1)
                            |
                         solve(0)
        
        At this point, the active stack contains approximately:        
                solve(3)
                solve(2)
                solve(1)
                solve(0)
                
        That's only O(n) stack frames.        
            Then solve(0) returns.
        
        The stack starts unwinding:        
              solve(0) returns
              solve(1) returns
        
        Then the second branch of solve(1) executes.
        
        So we never have the entire 2ⁿ recursion tree stored in memory simultaneously.
        
        Therefore:        
              Auxiliary Space = O(n)              
              because the maximum recursion depth is n.


Remember: ****************

          Time depends on how many total nodes we visit in the recursion tree.
          
          Space depends primarily on the maximum depth of the active call stack.



5. The key distinction

          This is extremely important for your Google interview:
          
          Concept	Complexity
          Number of recursive calls	O(2ⁿ)
          Recursion depth	O(n)
          Time	O(2ⁿ)
          Auxiliary space	O(n)
================================================================================================================================================
⭐ L5 Interview Answer

If the interviewer asks:

"What is the time and space complexity?"

A strong answer would be:

        "The recurrence is T(n) = 2T(n-1) + O(1), 
            because each invocation makes two recursive calls on n-1. 
            This produces a binary recursion tree with O(2ⁿ) nodes, 
            so the time complexity is O(2ⁿ).
        
        The auxiliary space is O(n), not O(2ⁿ), 
          because although the recursion tree contains exponentially many total calls, 
          only one root-to-leaf path is active at a time. 
          The maximum recursion depth is n, so the JVM call stack requires O(n) space."

That is the explanation I want you to be comfortable giving in an L5 interview.



